%I A399939 #5 Oct 05 2026 13:47:06
%S A399939 1,1,2,6,20,106,662,4724,37058,319804,3279088,36723872,439290400,
%T A399939 5782555030
%N A399939 Number of permutations of 1..n such that the Hankel matrix of any subsequence of an odd number of consecutive terms is nonsingular.
%C A399939 a(n) is even for n >= 2, because a permutation and its reversal are either both counted or both not counted.
%F A399939 a(n) = n!-A399940(n).
%e A399939 For n = 4, the permutations 1243, 3124, 3421, and 4213 are excluded because the Hankel matrix of the subsequence 124 (or 421), [[1,2],[2,4]], is singular. No other permutations are excluded, so a(4) = 4!-4 = 20.
%o A399939 (Python)
%o A399939 from itertools import permutations
%o A399939 from sympy import Matrix
%o A399939 def A399939(n):
%o A399939     return sum(1 for p in permutations(range(1,n+1)) if all(Matrix(m,m,lambda i,j:p[k+i+j]).det() for m in range(2,(n+3)//2) for k in range(n-2*m+2)))
%Y A399939 Cf. A000142, A350364, A399940.
%K A399939 nonn,more,new
%O A399939 0,3
